  "question": {
    "text": "In the interval \\((-2 \\pi, 0)\\), the function \\(f(x)=\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\)",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "24YlT",
      "text": "never changes sign",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "0K5jC",
      "text": "changes sign only once",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "6btNr",
      "text": "changes sign more than once but finitely many times",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qTA8o",
      "text": "changes sign infinitely many times",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint : \\(x \\in(-2 \\pi, 0), f(x)=\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\)\n\\(\\begin{aligned}\n& \\because-2 \\pi  < x < 0 \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow-8 \\pi^3 < x^3 < 0 \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow-\\infty < \\frac{1}{x^3} < -\\frac{1}{8 \\pi^3}\n\\end{aligned}\\)\nHence, \\(\\sin \\left(\\frac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\) will take all values from -1 to 1 , and will change its sign infinitely many times since it is a periodic function",
    "image": null
  },
